```sql
--Description / Thinking process:
--  • Goal: count how many unique breast-cancer adenocarcinoma samples (Morphology = '3111' and
--    Topography = '0401') have at least one genomic segment in CytoConverted.
--  • A “sample” is uniquely identified by (RefNo, CaseNo); we count DISTINCT combinations.
--  • Ambiguity arises because RefNo and CaseNo exist in both tables, so we fully qualify them
--    with table aliases: cc = CytoConverted, cg = Cytogen.
--  • Steps:
--      1. Join CytoConverted (cc) to Cytogen (cg) on RefNo & CaseNo.
--      2. Filter cg rows to breast-carcinoma adenocarcinoma (Morph = '3111', Topo = '0401').
--      3. Count DISTINCT concatenations of cc.RefNo and cc.CaseNo.
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(cc.RefNo, '_', cc.CaseNo)) AS total_samples
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS cg
  ON cc.RefNo  = cg.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = cg.CaseNo
WHERE cg.Morph = '3111'
  AND cg.Topo  = '0401';
